{# templates/compose/_send_pill.html.twig #}

{#
The Send pill, and behind its chevron, send later.

Included TWICE by _window.html.twig and rendered ONCE at any given width:

  • `placement: 'header'` — `md:hidden`, in the window header, where the phone
    expects it. This is the spot the old plain Send ICON held before the last
    round removed it; the pill itself lives there now, so scheduling is a thing
    a phone can do and there is still exactly one Send on screen.
  • `placement: 'bar'`   — `hidden md:flex`, first in the action bar, which is
    where the desktop has always had it and where it stays.

The two are the same markup on purpose. The previous shape of this — a header
icon that could only send and a bar pill that could also schedule — was two
Send buttons that disagreed about what Send meant. One partial cannot drift
into that.

**Both directions of the menu are here.** The bar pill opens UPWARD
(`bottom-full`) out of a window that already sits at the bottom of the screen,
anchored `left-0` because the pill is the first thing in the bar and a
right-anchored menu hung 150px off the window's left edge. The header pill is
the mirror image: it opens DOWNWARD (`top-full`, or the menu clears the top of
the phone) and anchors `right-0`, because in the header the pill is the LAST
thing on the row and a left-anchored `w-64` runs off the right of a 320px
screen. `max-w` keeps the narrow end honest either way.

The positioning box is OUTSIDE the pill because the pill is `overflow-hidden`
(that is what rounds the two buttons into one shape) and a menu absolutely
positioned inside it would be clipped to a 30px strip. Same shape as the
integration picker: relative wrapper, ui--dropdown, a `menu` target that starts
hidden.

Both halves take a 44px minimum below md. The chevron in particular was an 11px
hit area at its natural size, which is a control you aim at rather than press.

Every item is a real submit button pointed at the schedule route with
`formaction`, so choosing a time goes through the same submit path as Send —
the same guards in compose--compose#_handleSubmit, the same CSRF token, the
same form body. See compose--schedule.

Variables: message, params (from the including context); placement.
#}
{% set placement = placement|default('bar') %}
{% set header    = 'header' == placement %}

{% set scheduleUrl = null == message.id
    ? path('app_compose_mail_schedule', params)
    : path('app_compose_mail_schedule_draft', params|merge({'id': message.id})) %}
{# The hold already in place, if any. sentAt guards it: on a sent message
   submissionSendAt is a record of when it was due, not a schedule anyone can
   still call off. #}
{% set scheduledAt = (message.id and message.sentAt is null)
    ? message.submissionSendAt : null %}

<div class="relative shrink-0 {{ header ? 'flex md:hidden ml-1' : 'hidden md:flex mr-2' }}"
     data-compose-send-pill="{{ placement }}"
     data-controller="ui--dropdown compose--schedule"
     data-action="ui--dropdown:opened->compose--schedule#refresh"
     {# The zone every other timestamp in this app is drawn in — the user's
        setting, not the browser's guess. The menu computes "tomorrow morning"
        against it and posts a wall clock; ScheduledSendResolver turns that into
        an instant with the tz database in hand. #}
     data-compose--schedule-timezone-value="{{ clock.timezone }}"
     data-compose--schedule-max-days-value="{{ constant('App\\Service\\Mail\\ScheduledSendResolver::MAX_SECONDS') // 86400 }}"
     data-compose--schedule-i18n-value="{{ {
         scheduleUnreadable: 'compose.schedule.error.unreadable'|trans,
         schedulePast:       'compose.schedule.error.past'|trans,
         scheduleTooFar:     'compose.schedule.error.too_far'|trans({
             '%days%': constant('App\\Service\\Mail\\ScheduledSendResolver::MAX_SECONDS') // 86400,
         }),
     }|json_encode|e('html_attr') }}">

    <div class="flex items-center rounded-full overflow-hidden bg-accent">
        <button type="submit"
                data-compose--compose-target="sendBtn"
                class="flex items-center justify-center text-white text-sm font-semibold
                       px-4 py-1.5 max-md:min-h-11 hover:bg-white/10 transition-colors">
            {{ 'compose.send'|trans }}
        </button>
        <button type="button"
                data-action="click->ui--dropdown#toggle"
                title="{{ 'compose.send_options'|trans }}"
                aria-label="{{ 'compose.send_options'|trans }}"
                class="flex items-center justify-center text-white border-l border-white/30
                       px-2.5 py-1.5 max-md:min-h-11 max-md:min-w-11
                       hover:bg-white/10 transition-colors text-xs">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down" aria-hidden="true"></i>
        </button>
    </div>

    {# See the note at the top for why the direction and the anchor both flip
       with the placement. compose-scheduled-send.spec.ts measures the box that
       comes out of it at 393px and at 320px. #}
    <div data-ui--dropdown-target="menu" hidden
         class="absolute z-20 w-64 max-w-[calc(100vw-2rem)] popover py-1
                {{ header ? 'top-full mt-1 right-0 left-auto' : 'bottom-full mb-1 left-0 right-auto' }}">

        {% if scheduledAt is not null %}
            {# A draft that is already on a hold. The menu is where the schedule
               was set, so it is where it can be called off — and unlike the
               toast, this is still here tomorrow. #}
            <div class="px-3 py-2 border-b border-line">
                <p class="text-[10px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-ink-faint">
                    {{ 'compose.schedule.scheduled_for'|trans }}
                </p>
                <p class="text-sm text-ink mt-0.5">
                    {{ 'compose.schedule.when'|trans({
                        '%date%': scheduledAt|intl_date('dMMM', clock.timezone),
                        '%time%': scheduledAt|date(clock.time, clock.timezone),
                    }) }}
                </p>
                <button type="button"
                        data-cancel-url="{{ path('app_compose_mail_undo', params|merge({'id': message.id})) }}"
                        data-action="click->compose--schedule#cancel click->ui--dropdown#close"
                        class="mt-1 text-xs text-danger hover:underline cursor-pointer">
                    {{ 'compose.schedule.cancel'|trans }}
                </button>
            </div>
        {% endif %}

        <p class="px-3 py-1 text-[10px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-ink-faint">
            {{ 'compose.schedule.heading'|trans }}
        </p>

        {# Hidden until the controller has a time to put on it: a preset showing
           its label and no hour is a button whose effect you cannot read.

           `style="display:none"` as well as the attribute, and not belt and
           braces — `[hidden]` lives in the UA stylesheet, so ANY author rule
           that sets display wins over it, and `flex` below is exactly such a
           rule. The attribute stays for the semantics an assistive technology
           reads; the inline style is what actually keeps it off the screen.
           Kept in step by compose--schedule. #}
        {% for key in ['tomorrow_morning', 'tomorrow_afternoon', 'monday_morning'] %}
            <button type="submit" hidden style="display:none" formnovalidate
                    formaction="{{ scheduleUrl }}"
                    data-compose--schedule-target="option"
                    data-schedule-key="{{ key }}"
                    data-action="click->compose--schedule#choose"
                    class="w-full flex items-center justify-between gap-3 px-3 py-2 text-sm text-ink-muted
                           hover:bg-hover hover:text-ink transition-colors text-left">
                <span>{{ ('compose.schedule.' ~ key)|trans }}</span>
                <span data-schedule-when class="text-xs text-ink-faint tabular-nums"></span>
            </button>
        {% endfor %}

        <div class="my-1 border-t border-line"></div>

        <button type="button"
                data-compose--schedule-target="toggle"
                data-action="click->compose--schedule#showCustom"
                class="w-full flex items-center gap-2.5 px-3 py-2 text-sm text-ink-muted
                       hover:bg-hover hover:text-ink transition-colors text-left">
            <i class="fa-regular fa-calendar w-4 text-center" aria-hidden="true"></i>
            <span>{{ 'compose.schedule.custom'|trans }}</span>
        </button>

        {# Native input, no picker library — the same choice the calendar event
           editor makes. #}
        <div data-compose--schedule-target="custom" hidden class="px-3 py-2 space-y-2">
            <input type="datetime-local"
                   data-compose--schedule-target="input"
                   aria-label="{{ 'compose.schedule.custom'|trans }}"
                   class="w-full rounded-lg border border-line bg-surface px-2 py-1.5 text-sm text-ink">
            {# Named, because a bare time is ambiguous the moment the machine and
               the setting disagree — which is exactly when it matters. #}
            <p class="text-[11px] text-ink-faint">
                {{ 'compose.schedule.timezone_hint'|trans({ '%zone%': clock.timezone }) }}
            </p>
            <p data-compose--schedule-target="error" hidden
               class="text-xs text-danger"></p>
            <button type="submit" formnovalidate
                    formaction="{{ scheduleUrl }}"
                    data-action="click->compose--schedule#confirm"
                    class="w-full rounded-full bg-accent text-white text-sm font-semibold
                           px-3 py-1.5 hover:bg-white/10 transition-colors">
                {{ 'compose.schedule.confirm'|trans }}
            </button>
        </div>

        {# The only field of the lot that is submitted. The datetime-local above
           is deliberately unnamed — the value that counts is the one a preset or
           the confirm button writes here.

           **`disabled` until a time is actually chosen**, which is new with the
           second placement. There are two of these in the form now, both named
           `schedule_at`, and a disabled control is not submitted at all: without
           it PHP would take the LAST `schedule_at` in document order, which is
           the pill the user did not touch, and every schedule set from the
           header would post an empty time. compose--schedule arms its own field
           as it writes to it. #}
        <input type="hidden" name="schedule_at" value="" disabled
               data-compose--schedule-target="field">
    </div>
</div>
